sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design: B6 slot design (migration-dashboard/drafts/task-vm-slots.md, M1), docs/reference/compute-drivers.md (Task VM slots), docs/reference/task-vm-native-provision.md, DECISIONS R1-R4.
  %% Tightened: flow-tighten rules 1, 4, 5, 9, 14, 16, 17, 19 (no Node removal, no key revoke and no hub write: the slot keeps its Node, peer, volume and secrets; the Gateway scrubs its own records after the terminal result; the forced deletes need no read first; one end check).
  %% status: reviewed
  %% command: none · release the slot of a task
  %% kind: task_vm.slot_release
  autonumber
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane H icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>workflow execution
  participant H as Task VM host<br/>over ssh
  Note over GW: Validate, record release
  %%> Started by:
  %%> - the end of the task: it completed, was cancelled or failed, or its workspace was removed
  %%> - the task VM host sweep, for a slot that is `destroying` or `failed`
  %%> - there is no CLI command of its own
  %%> Requirements:
  %%> - no other task holds the slot; a repeated admission returns the same open task
  %%> - the workspace teardown has run in the guest, and approved work is published
  %%> - the slot is `ready`, `parked`, `provisioning`, `failed` or `destroying`
  %%> Result:
  %%> - the slot is `destroying`
  %%> - a task is recorded with the VM name and the seed clone name
  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-task-vm-slot-release
  %%> Inputs:
  %%> - the slot name, host, project and pool, and the two names to delete
  %%> Secrets:
  %%> - none; the release rotates no token, key or secret
  SW->>H: Delete the VM<br/>ssh exec · incus delete --force
  %%> How:
  %%> - one argv, for a running or a stopped VM
  %%> - an absent VM counts as done
  %%> Cancel:
  %%> - after this step succeeds, the task can no longer be cancelled
  SW->>H: Delete the seed clone<br/>ssh exec · incus volume delete
  %%> How:
  %%> - one argv, after the VM released the volume; an absent volume counts as done
  %%> Not included:
  %%> - the identity volume stays: the next claim uses it
  SW->>H: Read the host again<br/>ssh exec · forEach of 2
  %%> How:
  %%> - two parallel reads: the instances and the custom volumes of the project
  Note over SW: Check the slot is clean<br/>assert
  %%> End check:
  %%> - a native `assert` over the two reads
  %%> - neither the VM nor the seed clone is listed
  %%> - the identity volume is listed
  alt every step passes
    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event
    %%> How the Gateway learns it:
    %%> - its run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway
    Note over GW: Scrub, reset the slot
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - deletes the slot Node's agent facts of every kind: `host`, `units`, `checkout`, `checkouts`, `packages`, `ufw`
    %%> - removes the workspace records offline: Instance, Route, Schedule, Process except `pi-server`
    %%> - asserts the Node holds no Instance, Route, Schedule or Process except `pi-server`, no database connection, no firewall rule and no Tool
    %%> - clears the task, `claimed_at`, the image and the publish, topology and error fields
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the slot becomes `free`; the task succeeds
    %%> - the Node, the hub peer, the identity volume, the Pi token and the model key stay as they were
    %%> If the assert finds a leftover:
    %%> - the slot becomes `failed` and an alert fires; the task fails
    %%> Why scrub the facts:
    %%> - the next task uses the same workspace path, and an old checkout fact would still read as current
  else a step fails
    H--xSW: exit ≠ 0 or timeout
    %%> When:
    %%> - a delete, a read or the end check fails
    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step
    Note over GW: Keep the slot destroying
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the slot stays `destroying` with the error; no claim can take it
    %%> - the task fails
    %%> Retry:
    %%> - the host sweep admits the slot again; the forced deletes make the rerun safe
  end
